<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<section xml:id="ch7-section-thermodynamic-energy-equation" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
  <title>Thermodynamic energy equation</title>

  <p>
    The <term>thermodynamic energy equation</term>
    (also called the <term>thermal energy equation</term>)
    is produced by dividing the <m>1^{\text{st}}</m> law of thermodynamics for a perfectly dry air parcel
    given by <xref ref="eqn-first-law" text="type-global" />
    by an infinitesimal time interval <m>\delta t</m> and letting <m>\delta t \to 0</m>.
    The resulting equation is given by
  </p>

  <p>
    <md xml:id="eqn-thermodyanmic-energy-equation" number="yes">
        \dot{Q} = c_v \frac{DT}{Dt} + p \frac{D \alpha}{Dt}
    </md>
  </p>

  <p>
    where <m>\dot{Q}</m> [<m>\text{J} \, \text{kg}^{-1} \, \text{s}^{-1}</m>] is the <term>diabatic heating rate per unit mass</term>,
    which encompasses the rate at which an air parcel warms or cools due to radiation, conduction, latent heating, and/or frictional dissipation.
    Note that <m>J</m> (as in <xref ref="holton-hakim" text="custom">Holton and Hakim (2013)</xref>)
    often is used in place of <m>\dot{Q}</m>.
    We will adopt <m>\dot{Q}</m> going forward,
    consistent with <xref ref="martin" text="custom">Martin (2006)</xref>.
  </p>

  <p>
    The thermodynamic energy equation <xref ref="eqn-thermodyanmic-energy-equation" text="type-global" />
    says that the rate at which heat is added diabatically to a perfectly dry air parcel from its environment
    is proportional to the sum of the rate at which the air parcel's internal energy increases as it warms
    and the rate at which it does work on its environment by expanding.
  </p>

  <p>
    Performing the same steps on the related form of the <m>1^{\text{st}}</m> law of thermodynamics
    given by <xref ref="eqn-first-law-alt" text="type-global" /> produces
  </p>

  <p>
    <md xml:id="eqn-thermodynamic-energy-equation-alt" number="yes">
        \dot{Q} = c_p \frac{DT}{Dt} - \alpha \frac{Dp}{Dt}
    </md>
  </p>

  <p>
    The thermodynamic energy equation also can be written in terms of potential temperature.
    Differentiating the formula for the potential temperature of a perfectly dry air parcel
    given by <xref ref="eqn-potential-temperature" text="type-global" /> with respect to <m>t</m>,
    rearranging,
    and substituting the ideal gas law <xref ref="eqn-ideal-gas-law-specific-volume-dry" text="type-global" />
    produce
  </p>

  <p>
    <md xml:id="eqn-potential-temperature-derivative" number="yes">
        c_p \frac{DT}{Dt} - \alpha \frac{Dp}{Dt}
        =
        c_p \frac{T}{\theta} \frac{D \theta}{Dt}
    </md>
  </p>

  <p>
    Substituting <xref ref="eqn-potential-temperature-derivative" text="type-global" />
    into <xref ref="eqn-thermodynamic-energy-equation-alt" text="type-global" />
    and rearranging produces
  </p>

  <p>
    <md xml:id="eqn-thermodynamic-energy-equation-entropy-start" number="yes">
        \frac{\dot{Q}}{T}
        =
        \frac{c_p}{\theta}
        \frac{D \theta}{Dt}
    </md>
  </p>

  <p>
    Furthermore,
    substituting the differential of specific entropy <xref ref="eqn-entropy" text="type-global" />
    into <xref ref="eqn-thermodynamic-energy-equation-entropy-start" text="type-global" />
    produces
  </p>

  <p>
    <md xml:id="eqn-thermodynamic-energy-equation-entropy" number="yes">
        \frac{Ds}{Dt}
        =
        \frac{c_p}{\theta}
        \frac{D \theta}{Dt}
    </md>
  </p>

  <p>
    which leads to the relationship between specific entropy <m>s</m> and potential temperature <m>\theta</m>
  </p>

  <p>
    <md number="yes">
        s = c_p \ln{\theta}
    </md>
  </p>

  <p>
    when integrated over time.
    Thus,
    surfaces of constant potential temperature also are surfaces of constant specific entropy
    and so are called <term>isentropic surfaces</term>.
  </p>

  <p>
    For an adiabatic process, <xref ref="eqn-thermodynamic-energy-equation-entropy-start" text="type-global" />
    tells us <m>\dfrac{D \theta}{Dt} = 0</m>,
    which means <m>\theta</m> is conserved for a perfectly dry air parcel undergoing an adiabatic process.
    It further follows from <xref ref="eqn-thermodynamic-energy-equation-entropy" text="type-global" />
    that <m>\dfrac{Ds}{Dt} = 0</m> in this case,
    so <m>s</m> also is conserved.
    Thus, a reversible dry adiabatic process is called an <term>isentropic process</term>.
  </p>

</section>